<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Two years of progressive memory loss with getting lost while driving in a retired professional boxer of 63 with type 2 diabetes and hypertension; MoCA 18. Please stage the impairment, weigh Alzheimer&apos;s disease against traumatic encephalopathy syndrome and vascular disease, advise on biomarker testing and on eligibility for anti-amyloid therapy, and help us plan safety and support. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine; at the memory clinic two weeks from now, on the same day as his two-week primary care visit, with brain MRI and audiometry that morning. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What is the stage of his impairment, and is the pattern most consistent with Alzheimer&apos;s disease?</item><item>How should we think about traumatic encephalopathy syndrome and chronic traumatic encephalopathy after his boxing career, and about vascular disease?</item><item>Which biomarker test do you advise, and should APOE genotyping be done?</item><item>Is he a candidate for lecanemab or donanemab under the appropriate use recommendations?</item><item>What do you advise for symptomatic treatment, driving, firearm safety, hearing and support for his care partner?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Sugar Ray Robinson, a retired professional boxer of 63, is seen at the memory clinic with his care partner at the request of Dr. Pryce-Okonjo. He says his memory is not what it was but that this is normal at his age; his care partner describes two years of slowly worsening forgetfulness for recent events, repeated questions and stories, missed bills, and two episodes of getting lost while driving in familiar streets. There has been no sudden change, no hallucinations, no acting out of dreams, no falls and no change in personality, temper or mood. He boxed for about 25 years; he remembers many hard fights but cannot say how often he was dazed. He exercises daily and still goes to the gym of the youth foundation he started. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>He was born Walker Smith Jr. on May 3, 1921. The Michigan Sports Hall of Fame gives his birthplace as Ailey, Georgia, with the family later moving to Detroit; the 1989 UPI obituary says he was born in Detroit. [D]</item><item>As a boy he moved with his family to Harlem in New York, and he won the Golden Gloves featherweight title in 1939. [D]</item><item>He held the world welterweight championship, won in 1946, and the world middleweight title five times, first in 1951. [D]</item><item>He boxed professionally from 1940 to 1965, about 200 bouts in all: the 1989 UPI obituary counts 201 fights, 109 won by knockout and 65 by decision; the 1987 UPI report gives a record of 174-19-6 and the Michigan Sports Hall of Fame 173-19-6. [D]</item><item>After boxing he settled in Los Angeles, living modestly in the top story of a duplex on West Adams Boulevard, and in 1969 founded the Sugar Ray Robinson Youth Foundation for inner-city children in the Los Angeles area. [D]</item><item>The project roster describes his dementia as probable boxing-related dementia pugilistica; no source read records that diagnosis, chronic traumatic encephalopathy, or an autopsy finding. [R]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

