<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Amyloid PET to confirm Alzheimer&apos;s disease pathology; disclosure of the result with his care partner present if he wishes. [S]</item><item>Not a candidate for lecanemab or donanemab: more than four microbleeds on MRI; APOE genotyping not needed for treatment and offered only with genetic counseling. [S]</item><item>Traumatic encephalopathy syndrome recorded as a consideration only; it is a research diagnosis and does not change treatment. [S]</item><item>If amyloid is positive: donepezil 5 mg after a baseline ECG, raised to 10 mg after four to six weeks if tolerated; step back to 5 mg, or take it in the morning, for nausea, diarrhea, vivid dreams or a slow pulse. [S]</item><item>Stop glipizide and keep metformin, with a hemoglobin A1c goal under 8.0%. [S]</item><item>No driving; remove the firearm from the home. [S]</item><item>Hearing aids for both ears. [S]</item><item>Care partner education and respite, with the Alzheimer&apos;s Association 24/7 Helpline and an adult day program; the GUIDE dementia care model once he has Medicare. [S]</item><item>Advance care planning soon, while he can take part fully. [S]</item><item>MoCA and CDR yearly, or sooner with change; repeat MRI only for an acute change. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Pryce-Okonjo, who saw him the same day. The findings were explained to him and his care partner; he agreed to the amyloid PET, declined APOE testing, and agreed not to drive until the results visit. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Dr. Pryce-Okonjo will review the PET result with him at the six-week visit and continue his care; the memory clinic will see him again in a year, or sooner if he declines faster than expected or behavioral symptoms appear. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
